WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Third block.

2
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:05.000
A profile knows what each position prefers.

3
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.925
It still knows nothing about where the family tolerates a gap.

4
00:00:09.525 --> 00:00:14.600
Here is the gap problem drawn out, and it is a real mismatch with biology.

5
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:24.050
A PSSM gives position-specific substitution scores, but the gaps are handled by the alignment algorithm, with the same penalties everywhere.

6
00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:27.975
Every position is charged identically for an insertion.

7
00:00:27.975 --> 00:00:30.950
Real families are nothing like that.

8
00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:40.775
A loop between two helices may tolerate insertions of almost any length — the loop's job is to connect, and a few extra residues often connect just as well.

9
00:00:40.775 --> 00:00:48.950
The core of a domain almost never has one, because inserting a residue into a packed hydrophobic core means repacking the protein.

10
00:00:48.950 --> 00:01:03.175
So the same alignment carries positions where a gap costs essentially nothing biologically and positions where it is close to prohibited, and a uniform penalty splits the difference, charging too much in the loops and far too little in the core.

11
00:01:03.175 --> 00:01:08.325
Too much in the loops means missing real relatives that differ by a loop insertion.

12
00:01:08.325 --> 00:01:13.625
Too little in the core means accepting alignments no protein could actually realise.

13
00:01:13.625 --> 00:01:18.650
And note that the information needed to do better is already sitting in the alignment.

14
00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:23.825
If a column is full of gaps in the family, insertions are clearly tolerated there.

15
00:01:23.825 --> 00:01:28.400
The PSSM formalism simply has nowhere to put that fact.

16
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:33.150
The slide's alignment, by the way, is a schematic rather than real data.

17
00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:42.975
A profile hidden Markov model puts position-specific match, insertion and deletion behaviour into a single probabilistic model of the family.

18
00:01:42.975 --> 00:01:48.575
It uses three kinds of state, in a repeating unit, one unit per alignment position.

19
00:01:48.575 --> 00:01:51.875
Match states represent a conserved position.

20
00:01:51.875 --> 00:01:58.975
Each has its own per-residue emission probabilities, and plays exactly the role of one PSSM column.

21
00:01:58.975 --> 00:02:04.050
If a profile HMM had only match states, it would be a PSSM.

22
00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:08.950
Insert states represent extra residues between conserved positions.

23
00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:22.775
The model emits a residue from an insert state and can then loop back to the same state, which is how it allows insertions of more than one residue — and of unbounded length, with each extra residue costing another pass round the loop.

24
00:02:22.775 --> 00:02:26.550
Delete states represent a skipped conserved position.

25
00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:32.125
They emit nothing at all; they are a way to pass through the model without consuming a residue.

26
00:02:32.125 --> 00:02:35.275
Begin and End states bookend the whole thing.

27
00:02:35.275 --> 00:02:46.150
Transitions connect the states of consecutive positions — match to next match, match to insert, match to next delete, and so on — and every transition has its own probability.

28
00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:48.675
Here is the part that matters.

29
00:02:48.675 --> 00:02:53.500
Those transition probabilities are learned per position, from the family.

30
00:02:53.500 --> 00:03:01.625
So the model does not merely permit insertions; it knows where in this family insertions actually happen, and charges accordingly.

31
00:03:01.625 --> 00:03:05.625
A gap in a loop where the family routinely has gaps is cheap.

32
00:03:05.625 --> 00:03:09.750
The same gap in a conserved core position is expensive.

33
00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:14.375
That is precisely the information the PSSM could not express.

34
00:03:14.975 --> 00:03:19.275
A word on the name, since "hidden" sounds mysterious and is not.

35
00:03:19.275 --> 00:03:22.650
Take an observed residue in a real sequence.

36
00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:26.050
Did it come from a match state or an insert state?

37
00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:28.775
The model never tells you directly.

38
00:03:28.775 --> 00:03:31.325
All you see is the residue.

39
00:03:31.325 --> 00:03:43.625
Which state produced it has to be inferred — and it is inferred not residue by residue but from the path through the states that best explains the whole sequence, using emission and transition probabilities together.

40
00:03:43.625 --> 00:03:53.925
A residue near a region the family usually inserts into is probably an insert; the same residue where the family is rigidly conserved is probably a match.

41
00:03:53.925 --> 00:03:58.725
The evidence is the whole sequence and the whole model, not the one position.

42
00:03:58.725 --> 00:04:03.150
So the states generate what you observe, but they themselves stay hidden.

43
00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:09.375
That is the general meaning of "hidden" in any hidden Markov model, and it is the whole of the mystery.

44
00:04:09.375 --> 00:04:13.575
The two paths drawn on this slide are a schematic of that idea.

45
00:04:14.175 --> 00:04:16.800
To put the two side by side.

46
00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:25.075
A PSSM: one row per position, substitution scores only, gaps handled outside the model with uniform penalties.

47
00:04:25.075 --> 00:04:30.725
Simple, fast, and — as we saw — already a linear model you can read entry by entry.

48
00:04:30.725 --> 00:04:42.075
A profile HMM: match, insert and delete states per position, with learned transition probabilities, so insertions and deletions are part of the model rather than bolted on.

49
00:04:42.075 --> 00:04:44.950
The practical difference is concrete.

50
00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:56.675
A profile HMM can recognise a new family member even when it has a genuinely different number of residues in a variable region, because the model has learned that this region varies in length.

51
00:04:56.675 --> 00:05:01.750
A PSSM with uniform gap penalties can only approximate that.

52
00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:09.125
Which is why profile HMMs, not PSSMs, sit behind the protein family databases — Pfam above all.

53
00:05:09.125 --> 00:05:19.825
The tools are HMMER, with hmmsearch to search using a model and jackhmmer to iterate one from a single sequence, exactly as PSI-BLAST iterates a PSSM.

54
00:05:19.825 --> 00:05:35.850
And then HHblits, which goes a step further and aligns a profile against a database of profiles rather than against plain sequences — using family information on both sides at once, which reaches relationships neither BLAST nor a single-profile search will find.

55
00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:40.650
It is slower, which is why BLAST is still the default for everyday work.

56
00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:46.600
The same idea at production scale, on the protein we have followed since Session three.

57
00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:57.800
Search Pfam's library of profile HMMs against the real UniProt entry P68871, human beta-globin, fetched live from EBI's InterPro API.

58
00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:13.125
The search finds PF00042, the Globin family, covering residues twenty-seven to a hundred and forty-two of the hundred and forty-seven-residue protein, with an E-value of around one point three times ten to the minus twenty-nine.

59
00:06:13.125 --> 00:06:18.550
In a database of this size, essentially no match that good is expected by chance.

60
00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:20.900
Two things to take from it.

61
00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:29.875
The domain does not span the whole protein — it starts at residue twenty-seven, and the first twenty-six residues are not part of the globin domain.

62
00:06:29.875 --> 00:06:38.550
A profile HMM finds a domain within a sequence, not a whole-sequence resemblance, and real proteins are assemblies of domains.

63
00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:41.700
And compare this with where the block started.

64
00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:47.725
BLAST, with one sequence against a fixed matrix, gave myoglobin an E-value of sixty-three.

65
00:06:47.725 --> 00:06:56.075
A profile HMM, with a family behind it, gives beta-globin's own family membership an E-value of ten to the minus twenty-nine.

66
00:06:56.075 --> 00:06:59.025
That is the value of knowing the family.

67
00:06:59.025 --> 00:07:05.250
The lab does the same search on HIV-one's Gag-Pol polyprotein, through the same API.

68
00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:11.550
Now the limitation both models share, because it is the one that drives the rest of the course.

69
00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:14.850
Both score a sequence position by position.

70
00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:20.975
The score is a sum over columns, so every position contributes independently of every other.

71
00:07:20.975 --> 00:07:27.775
Whatever the profile has learned about position forty, it applies regardless of what sits at position ninety.

72
00:07:27.775 --> 00:07:30.950
Real proteins are not always like that.

73
00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:40.850
When mutations interact — when the effect of one depends on the residue at another position — the phenomenon is called epistasis, and no profile can represent it.

74
00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:50.225
A sum of independent terms cannot express "these two are fine individually and disastrous together", because the sum does not know they occurred together.

75
00:07:50.225 --> 00:07:53.700
This matters concretely, not just in principle.

76
00:07:53.700 --> 00:08:05.175
Session nine shows a protein where it dominates: all hundred and sixty thousand variants at four positions of GB1, where the effect of a mutation genuinely depends on its partners.

77
00:08:05.175 --> 00:08:17.675
Session twelve shows how pairwise models fitted to an alignment — direct coupling analysis — add those interactions back, and why that turned out to be the key to predicting contacts and eventually structures.

78
00:08:17.675 --> 00:08:32.875
And the same limitation in its simplest possible form is Session seven's XOR problem: a function no sum of independent weighted inputs can compute, which is exactly why a single perceptron is not enough and why networks have hidden layers.

79
00:08:32.875 --> 00:08:37.350
The profile's limitation and the perceptron's are the same limitation.

80
00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:46.725
Rather than take any of that on trust, here is a real profile HMM opened up — Pfam's Globin model, the same file HMMER uses.

81
00:08:46.725 --> 00:08:53.725
The top panel shows, for each of the hundred and seventeen match states, the probability of each amino acid.

82
00:08:53.725 --> 00:08:59.650
Each match state carries twenty emission probabilities, stored as negative natural logs.

83
00:08:59.650 --> 00:09:11.175
A few columns are almost fixed — you can see one histidine and three near-invariant positions — and those are the residues Session four's logo identified as the ones globins cannot do without.

84
00:09:11.175 --> 00:09:13.650
But look at most of the columns.

85
00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:15.850
They are far from fixed.

86
00:09:15.850 --> 00:09:22.100
The model is recording a distribution over residues at each position, not a consensus letter.

87
00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:26.950
That is the thing a consensus sequence throws away and a profile keeps.

88
00:09:26.950 --> 00:09:32.875
The bottom panel is the transitions, and this is what a PSSM cannot express at all.

89
00:09:32.875 --> 00:09:36.825
Each match state has seven transition probabilities.

90
00:09:36.825 --> 00:09:49.575
The typical match-to-insert probability is about nought point zero zero four and match-to-delete about nought point zero zero two — so for most of the model, insertions and deletions are strongly discouraged.

91
00:09:49.575 --> 00:09:54.950
At a few positions, though, they rise to between nought point one and nought point seven.

92
00:09:54.950 --> 00:09:59.575
A hundred-fold increase, concentrated at specific positions.

93
00:09:59.575 --> 00:10:04.675
Those are the loops between helices, where globins genuinely differ in length.

94
00:10:04.675 --> 00:10:08.325
That is the whole argument of this block in one picture.

95
00:10:08.325 --> 00:10:15.075
A PSSM charges the same gap penalty at every one of those hundred and seventeen positions.

96
00:10:15.075 --> 00:10:24.550
The HMM has learned, from the family, which handful of positions are the ones where length actually varies — and it got that for free from the same alignment.

97
00:10:25.150 --> 00:10:31.150
Which brings us back to the picture from the start of the session, and to a question I want to leave open.

98
00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:37.575
Everything in this session has been getting faster and more sensitive by knowing more about the family.

99
00:10:37.575 --> 00:10:56.575
And it continues: MMseqs2 on GPUs runs single-protein searches six times faster than CPU methods on two by sixty-four cores, speeds up ColabFold's structure prediction by thirty-one point eight times over the standard AlphaFold2 pipeline, and Foldseek by four to twenty-seven times.

100
00:10:56.575 --> 00:11:00.150
But alongside that, something different is happening.

101
00:11:00.150 --> 00:11:12.625
Profiles are being replaced by embedding vectors from protein language models — Session eleven — where a sequence becomes a few hundred numbers and two sequences are compared by the distance between their vectors.

102
00:11:12.625 --> 00:11:15.575
It works, often better than a profile.

103
00:11:15.575 --> 00:11:18.350
Now hold the two side by side.

104
00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:23.800
The profile HMM on the previous slide can be read and checked, column by column.

105
00:11:23.800 --> 00:11:30.200
You can look at match state ninety-two, see that it is almost all histidine, and connect it to the haem iron.

106
00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:36.175
You can look at the transition probabilities, find the loops, and check them against the known structure.

107
00:11:36.175 --> 00:11:41.100
If the model is wrong about a position, you can point at the number that is wrong.

108
00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:43.950
An embedding cannot be read that way.

109
00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:46.575
There is no column ninety-two.

110
00:11:46.575 --> 00:11:50.125
There is no entry you can check against the chemistry.

111
00:11:50.125 --> 00:11:53.750
So the question: what do we gain, and what do we lose?

112
00:11:53.750 --> 00:11:57.975
We gain sensitivity and speed, which are not small things.

113
00:11:57.975 --> 00:12:04.575
We lose the ability to inspect, to check against what we know, and to say why the model thinks what it thinks.

114
00:12:04.575 --> 00:12:13.050
This is the same question Session four ended on with learned similarity in place of a substitution matrix, and it will keep coming back.

115
00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:16.025
I do not think it has a settled answer.

116
00:12:16.625 --> 00:12:18.975
To draw the session together.

117
00:12:18.975 --> 00:12:25.000
BLAST: fast, one matrix for every position, E-values tied to the database it searched.

118
00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:31.950
It trades optimality for speed, and we saw it skip a real myoglobin alignment that was sitting there to be found.

119
00:12:31.950 --> 00:12:40.250
PSI-BLAST: a PSSM refined by iteration, so the scoring becomes specific to the family rather than generic.

120
00:12:40.250 --> 00:12:49.650
More sensitive to remote homologs, and vulnerable to profile drift, because a model that selects its own training data can reinforce its own mistakes.

121
00:12:49.650 --> 00:12:58.825
Profile HMMs: match, insert and delete states with learned transition probabilities, so the model knows where the family tolerates gaps.

122
00:12:58.825 --> 00:13:05.800
This is what Pfam is built from, and what finds a globin domain in a real sequence at ten to the minus twenty-nine.

123
00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:15.725
The thread, as promised at the start: the more the search knows about the family, the more distant the relatives it can find — and the more carefully the family has to be built.

124
00:13:15.725 --> 00:13:20.150
BLAST knows nothing about the family and misses distant relatives.

125
00:13:20.150 --> 00:13:23.875
A profile HMM knows a great deal and finds them.

126
00:13:23.875 --> 00:13:30.125
In between sits PSI-BLAST, which builds its own family and can therefore build the wrong one.

127
00:13:30.125 --> 00:13:35.225
And all three share a limitation: each position is scored independently.

128
00:13:35.225 --> 00:13:39.350
Interactions between positions are invisible to all of them.

129
00:13:39.950 --> 00:13:42.150
That is Session five.

130
00:13:42.150 --> 00:13:58.500
The notebook reproduces every worked example in code — it traces the X-drop extension, builds the PSSM from the toy alignment with and without pseudocounts including the rare-residue check, and fetches beta-globin's real Pfam hit live from the InterPro API.

131
00:13:58.500 --> 00:14:23.075
The lab identifies an unknown gene with a real NCBI BLAST search — it turns out to be a sixth human globin-family gene, continuing the thread — then builds a Clustal Omega alignment and PSSM of five globin-family proteins, compares round one with round three of an iterated profile search with jackhmmer against every sequence in the PDB, and searches Pfam for HIV-one's Gag-Pol polyprotein.

132
00:14:23.075 --> 00:14:31.225
Every search runs on EMBL-EBI's servers through web APIs, so there is nothing to install and it all runs on Colab.

133
00:14:31.225 --> 00:14:34.625
Session six follows the wall this session hit.

134
00:14:34.625 --> 00:14:44.600
A remote homolog can become unrecognisable from its sequence while its fold stays conserved — the bacterial truncated haemoglobins being the case in point.

135
00:14:44.600 --> 00:14:54.525
So Session six turns to structure itself: the four levels of protein structure, hands-on in PyMOL, structure alignment, and structural search with Foldseek.

136
00:14:54.525 --> 00:14:56.150
See you there.

